 only picking out the very  peak motivation moments of the animals  and it might be that these animals are  making responses where there are still  maybe a little bit little bit to drink  for just not very much we don't care  that much or it might be that they're  actually exploring at that point and not  trying to make a response that might be  difficult to distinguish but I think at  least the advantage that this gives is  if you think about that that water  reward visual discriminating again where  we looked at how long since the last  print one thing that this lets you do is  to examine the factors that determine  performance like how long it's been  since a mouse last strand and then go on  when the mouse hasn't had a drink when  the mouse has had a drink within the  last minute this data is basically  garbage and then I didn't go in there  and weed out that data and only keep the  data where the animals are at the  motivation so the animals can help us  along by picking their most motivated  type coins and we can do a bit of the  rest basically I would like to once  again thank Jamie for his presentation  I'd also like to thank lab rats for  making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I'd like to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through May of 2018 you will receive an  email from laborat letting you know when  this webcast will be available for  replay announcement with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and we hope to see you again soon  goodbye goodbye thanks again Alexis and  right on
 

